# Answer 6 · Two Western Schools of Feminist Thought: Liberal and Radical Feminism (CSS 2022)
@py question 2022-3

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Choose two contrasting schools and describe each fully — meaning, origin, thinkers, ideas, achievements, criticisms — then compare them and apply them to Pakistan.
@ask DESCRIBE any TWO WESTERN SCHOOLS of thought about feminism IN DETAIL
(1) Introduction: feminism is a family of schools; liberal reforms, radical transforms; why these two
(2) Liberal feminism, meaning: women are rational individuals; remove legal and customary barriers
(3) Liberal origins and thinkers: Wollstonecraft 1792, Mill 1869, Friedan 1963, NOW 1966
(4) Liberal ideas and achievements: equal rights and education; the vote, equal pay, CEDAW
(5) Liberal criticisms: ignores structures and the family; male norm; middle-class bias
(6) Radical feminism, meaning: patriarchy is the root of oppression; change it at the root
(7) Radical origins and thinkers: New Left 1960s; Millett, Firestone, Hanisch, Brownmiller
(8) Radical ideas and achievements: the personal is political; shelters, rape and harassment law
(9) Radical criticisms: ignores race and class; essentialism; anti-men image
(10) The two compared: reform versus transformation; public versus private
(11) Pakistan: liberal in the Constitution and laws; radical in the fight against violence
(12) Conclusion: the two need each other
:::

## Decoding the question
**"Describe … in detail"** asks for full, organised description — not a list of names. **"Any two"** lets you choose: pick two schools that **contrast** well and that you know deeply. **Liberal** and **radical** feminism are the best pair: the first is the most influential in law, the second the most influential on violence against women. Describe each under the **same sub-headings**, then add a short comparison and a Pakistan angle — that is what "in detail" earns.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**Feminism** is the belief in **equal rights and chances** for women and men, with **action** to achieve it. Western feminist thought has produced several **schools** — liberal, radical, Marxist, socialist, psychoanalytic and postmodern — each explaining women's disadvantage differently.

#### ii) The two schools chosen
This answer describes **liberal feminism**, which seeks **reform** within the existing system, and **radical feminism**, which seeks **transformation** at the root. Together they show the range of Western feminist thought.

### 2. Liberal feminism — meaning

#### i) Definition
**Liberal feminism** holds that women are as capable of **reason** as men, and that their disadvantage comes from **unequal laws, customs and opportunities**. The remedy is **equal rights, equal education and equal access** to work and public life.

#### ii) Its roots
It grows from **Enlightenment liberalism**: every **individual** has reason and **natural rights**. Liberal feminists ask why these rights should stop at women.

#### iii) In simple words
If a girl is as able as her brother, she should have the **same school, the same vote, the same chance at a job** and the **same protection of law**. Remove the barriers, and she will rise on merit.

### 3. Liberal feminism — origins and thinkers

#### i) First wave
**Mary Wollstonecraft** (*A Vindication of the Rights of Woman*, ==1792==) answered **Rousseau**: women seem weak because they are denied a **rational education**. At **Seneca Falls** (==1848==) the **Declaration of Sentiments** declared "all men and women are created equal". **John Stuart Mill** (*The Subjection of Women*, ==1869==), with **Harriet Taylor Mill**, called the legal subordination of women wrong in itself and a barrier to progress.

#### ii) Second wave
**Betty Friedan** (*The Feminine Mystique*, ==1963==) described "**the problem that has no name**" — the emptiness of educated housewives — and co-founded the **National Organization for Women** (==1966==).

#### iii) Later
**Susan Moller Okin** (==1989==) argued that the **family must be just**; **Martha Nussbaum** (==2000==) applied the **capabilities approach** to women.

### 4. Liberal feminism — ideas and achievements

#### i) Core ideas
**Individual rights**, **equality of opportunity**, **education**, **reform through law** and focus on the **public sphere**.

#### ii) Achievements
The **vote** (New Zealand ==1893==, USA ==1920==, Britain ==1928==), access to **universities and professions**, the US **Equal Pay Act** (==1963==), and the UN **CEDAW** (==1979==), which Pakistan ratified in ==1996==. US law also banned **sex discrimination in employment** (Title VII, ==1964==).

#### iii) Methods
Liberal feminists work through **lobbying, lawsuits, legislation and elections** — the ordinary tools of a democracy. This makes the school **practical** and widely acceptable.

### 5. Liberal feminism — criticisms

#### i) Structures ignored
Equal laws do not produce equal results while women do most **unpaid care**.

#### ii) The private sphere
It keeps the state out of the **family**, where much inequality lives.

#### iii) A male norm and a class bias
It asks women to become like men in a system built around men's lives; **bell hooks** said Friedan spoke only for **educated white housewives**, while poor and Black women had always worked.

#### iv) Individualism
It focuses on **individual merit and choice**, and underplays the **group disadvantage** that holds whole classes of women back.

### 6. Radical feminism — meaning

#### i) Definition
"Radical" comes from Latin ***radix***, **root**. **Radical feminism** holds that **patriarchy** — male power over women — is the **oldest and deepest** oppression, found in every area of life, above all the **family, sexuality, the body and violence**.

#### ii) Its remedy
Reform is not enough; society and culture must be **transformed at the root**.

#### iii) In simple words
A new law against harassment helps, but the radical feminist asks **why men harass at all**. The answer — a culture that treats women's bodies as men's to use — must itself be changed.

### 7. Radical feminism — origins and thinkers

#### i) Origin
It arose in the USA in the **late 1960s**, when women in the civil-rights and anti-war "**New Left**" found themselves sidelined by male comrades. Groups such as **New York Radical Women** (==1967==) and **Redstockings** (==1969==) followed.

#### ii) Thinkers
**Kate Millett** (*Sexual Politics*, ==1970==) showed sex relations to be **power relations** and made "**patriarchy**" the key concept. **Shulamith Firestone** (*The Dialectic of Sex*, ==1970==) traced oppression to **reproduction** and called women a "**sex class**". **Carol Hanisch** gave the slogan "**The Personal Is Political**" (==1970==). **Susan Brownmiller** (==1975==) showed **rape** as a tool of fear. **Adrienne Rich** (==1976==, ==1980==) separated motherhood as an **experience** from motherhood as an **institution** controlled by men.

#### iii) Two branches
**Radical-libertarian** feminists (Firestone) wanted to free people from rigid gender; **radical-cultural** feminists (Mary Daly) wanted to **revalue** women's qualities of care and peace.

### 8. Radical feminism — ideas and achievements

#### i) Core ideas
**Patriarchy**, **sexual politics**, **the personal is political**, **consciousness-raising** (women sharing experiences to see their political cause) and **bodily autonomy**.

#### ii) Achievements
**Rape crisis centres** and **women's shelters** (London, ==1971==); **domestic violence** redefined as a public crime; **sexual harassment** recognised as discrimination (**Catharine MacKinnon**, ==1979==).

### 9. Radical feminism — criticisms

#### i) One "woman"
It treated women as one group, ignoring **race and class** (hooks, Audre Lorde).

#### ii) Essentialism and image
Firestone's focus on biology risks **essentialism**; separatist writing gave it an **anti-men** image; the 1980s "**sex wars**" divided the movement.

### 10. The two compared

::: fig compare | Liberal and radical feminism side by side.
## Liberal feminism
- Cause: unequal laws and chances
- Remedy: reform within the system
- Sphere: public — law, work, politics
- Method: lobbying, courts, elections
- Legacy: the vote, equal pay, CEDAW
## Radical feminism
- Cause: patriarchy itself
- Remedy: transform at the root
- Sphere: private and public — family, body
- Method: consciousness-raising, protest
- Legacy: shelters, rape and harassment law
:::

#### i) Where they agree
Both agree that women's disadvantage is **made by society**, not by nature, and both have changed the law.

#### ii) Where they differ
They disagree on its **depth** and therefore on the **cure**: liberals see barriers to be removed; radicals see a system of power to be dismantled. Liberals speak to **parliaments**; radicals speak first to **women themselves**.

### 11. The Pakistan angle

#### i) Liberal
Pakistan's **Constitution** is liberal in spirit — **Articles 25, 27 and 34** — and laws on **harassment** (==2010==) and **child marriage** (==2025==) follow the liberal route.

#### ii) Radical
The **Women's Action Forum** (==1981==) against the **Hudood Ordinances**, the laws on **honour killing** (==2016==) and **rape** (==2021==), and the **Aurat March** demand for **bodily autonomy** reflect radical insight.

### 12. Conclusion
**Liberal feminism** built women's **legal equality**; **radical feminism** exposed the **power and violence** that laws alone could not reach. The first gives rights, the second shows why rights are not enough. In Pakistan, as elsewhere, the two **need each other**: liberal law provides the tools, radical analysis shows where to use them.

## Why this answer scores
- The choice of schools is **explained** — the examiner sees a thinking candidate.
- Both schools are described under **the same sub-headings**: meaning, origin and thinkers, ideas and achievements, criticisms — true "detail".
- **Thinkers, books and dates** are exact.
- A **compare figure** and a **Pakistan section** lift the answer above description.
